# Copy to apps/next/.env.local — Next and the db:* scripts load env files from apps/next, not
# from the repo root. Every variable is optional: with none set, the app boots, tests and runs on
# the in-memory adapters and console sinks.
#
# Secrets and per-deployment values only. Adapter selection, vector dimensions and distance metric
# are configured in code, at apps/next/src/server/db/runtime.ts.
#
# Leave a variable commented out rather than empty: DATABASE_URL="" fails URL validation.

# ── PostgreSQL ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read by apps/next/src/server/db/env.ts and drizzle.config.ts. Unset, `ctx.db` exists but its first
# query throws, and health.check fails; module import never connects.
# Any Postgres works (local, Neon, Supabase, RDS). Prepared statements are off, so a transaction-mode
# pooler URL (PgBouncer, Supabase port 6543) is fine.
# After setting it: `bun run db:migrate` applies apps/next/migrations.
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/app"

# ── Sentry (errors and logs) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sentry → Project Settings → Client Keys (DSN). Both are usually the same DSN.
# SENTRY_DSN is read at runtime by the server (src/instrumentation.ts). Unset, the SDK is inert.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN is inlined into the browser bundle at *build* time
# (src/instrumentation-client.ts), so it must be set when `bun run build` runs.
# Log records are routed to Sentry only when NODE_ENV=production; `bun run dev` logs to the console,
# though unhandled request errors still reach Sentry when a DSN is set.
# SENTRY_DSN="https://examplePublicKey@o0.ingest.sentry.io/0"
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN="https://examplePublicKey@o0.ingest.sentry.io/0"

# ── Metrics (OTLP/HTTP → Prometheus-compatible backend) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Read by apps/next/src/server/metrics/runtime.ts, only when NODE_ENV=production; `bun run dev`
# prints increments to the console. Unset in production, metrics are dropped.
# The endpoint is the base URL: /v1/metrics is appended. Headers use the standard OTel format,
# comma-separated key=value with URL-encoded values.
# Grafana Cloud: Stack → OpenTelemetry → Configure gives both values; the Authorization value is
# "Basic " + base64("<instance id>:<access policy token>"), with the space encoded as %20.
# Alert rules are not uploaded from here: load apps/next/rules.yaml with mimirtool or cortextool.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT="https://otlp-gateway-prod-us-central-0.grafana.net/otlp"
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS="Authorization=Basic%20BASE64_INSTANCE_ID_COLON_TOKEN"

# ── Demo webhook ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read by apps/next/src/server/webhooks/runtime.ts. POST /api/webhooks/demo accepts a delivery only
# when x-demo-signature is "sha256=" + the hex HMAC-SHA256 of the raw body under this secret.
# Unset, development falls back to a fixed dev secret and production answers 503. Give each real
# provider its own variable (STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET, …), read in the same file.
# DEMO_WEBHOOK_SECRET="a-long-random-string"

# ── Server ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The port `next start`/`next dev` listen on; server-side tRPC calls build their URL from it
# (src/routes.ts). Defaults to 3000.
# PORT=3000
    